Boudewijn Waijers wrote on Fri, 10 Dec 2004 18:01:38 +0100:
> Rast wrote:
> 
> > Check.  We can also include accomplishments not necessary for
> > ascension, like completing Sokoban and fetching the luckstone.
> 
> These accomplishments make ascension easier and more likely, so they
> already carry a reward in itself.

If two players have similar failed games but one beats Sokoban, he 
should have a higher score.

I don't think any of the criteria Damien mentioned should be used to 
score ascensions.

> > I would simply give a bonus for each character level from 2-13, and a
> > big bonus for level 14.  Small bonuses could be awarded for reaching
> > lvls 20, 25, 30; those aren't needed, but they do make your character
> > more powerful, so they count as progress towards ascension.
> 
> Why reward 14 more than other levels? Because it's a prerequisite for
> entering the Quest? Note that reaching level 13 is a prerequisite for
> reaching level 14, and thus a prerequisite for entering the Quest as
> well...

By that logic, descending to level two should be worth exactly the same 
as performing the invocation, since both are steps on the path to 
ascension...

Attaining XL14 is a major step towards the goal of going on the quest.  
Attaining XL13 is a minor step towards the goal of reaching XL14.  

I don't see a problem with making one of these be worth much more than 
the other.


> >> - Carnage score--points awarded for each monster killed.
> > I think this is already well-covered by giving points for experience
> > levels gained.
> 
> Not really. One of the reasons for changing the scoring system should
> be, in my opinion, to remove the link between scoring and experience.
> For example, I think that your experience should go up by the same
> amount for each monster of a certain type you killed, while I believe
> that your score should go up by a steadily decreasing amount for each
> monster of the same type killed.

Why should your score go up at all for killing monsters?  Despite the 
"hack" in the game's title, the game is driven my the quest to ascend.  
Killing monsters is an avoidable sidelight.  The player is already 
rewarded enough for kills (biggest reward: one less monster trying to 
kill you); a score bonus is not useful.

> >> - Wealth--points awarded for zorkmids of wealth owned at the end of
> >> the game, decreasing exponentially with higher numbers of zorkmids.
> > Again, why?
> 
> I can think of a reason: making choices. Do you want to spend lots of
> money on priests, gaining protection and other benefits, or would you
> rather keep your money, for a direct effect on your score? This could be
> a difficult choice, if you're playing for score.

It is trivial to have the best of both worlds in this scenario.  It is 
also trivial to accumulate unbounded amounts of valuable gems. 



